<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Chimor</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1470 CE (approx.) · capital Chan Chan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest kingdom of the Andean coast before the Incas, ruled from the vast adobe city of Chan Chan and stretching some 1,000 km along northern Peru.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chimor arose around 900 in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moche">Moche</a> valley, where the Moche had once ruled. According to a legend recorded by the Spanish, its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacaynamo">Tacaynamo</a> came by sea and established a dynasty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>. Later rulers expanded by conquest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancen-pinco">Ñancen Pinco</a> extended the kingdom over neighbouring valleys.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Chimú kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>, the largest adobe city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, with walled palace compounds called ciudadelas that each served a ruler in life and as his tomb after death. The state managed extensive irrigation canals, including one linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chicama-valley">Chicama</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moche">Moche</a> valleys. Chimú craftsmen excelled in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> work and black burnished pottery. Around 1375 Chimor conquered the lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sican">Sicán</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minchancaman">Minchancaman</a> it reached as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbes">Tumbes</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1470 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a> conquered Chimor, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minchancaman">Minchancaman</a> and moved its craftsmen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a> was later looted for gold by the Spanish. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1986.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-chimor.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Chimor" width="320" height="723"><figcaption>Image: Trustsongs · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photomontage_Chimu_Culture.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1470 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chan Chan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tacaynamo, Ñancen Pinco, Minchancaman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chimú religion, moon worship</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Muchik</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chimú, Chimor, Chimú Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1185&amp;focus=kingdom-of-chimor" aria-label="Kingdom of Chimor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2494 2481 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Chimor lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2814 2737L2790 2675M2814 2737L3027 2937"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2790 2675h0M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2814 2737h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2814 2737h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chan Chan. <a href="/atlas?year=1185&amp;focus=kingdom-of-chimor">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
